s were awarded to British writer " [:a {:href "/athletes/920551"} "Dorothy Margaret Stuart"] " and Danish author " [:a {:href "/athletes/920239"} "Josef Petersen"] ". Stuart received it for " [:em {} "Sword Songs"] ". She joined The English Association in 1930 and was a member of the Executive Committee from 1937-61, Honorary Secretary of the Association from 1949-51, and edited the " [:em {} "News-Letter"] " from it’s inception in 1946 to 1961. She was known for her literary criticism and for several historical biographies. Petersen wrote mostly about ancient and medieval motifs, and was well recognized for his knowledge of ancient cultures, but not from the contemporary literary criticism. He won all his Olympic art medals (1924, " [:a {:href "/results/920033"} "1932"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/920063"} "1948"] ") with works on ancient Greek sports motifs, in 1924 for " [:em {} "Euryale"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The bronze medals went to French " [:a {:href "/athletes/920333"} "Charles Anthoine Gonnet"] ", for " [:em {} "Face to Face with Olympia’s God"] ", and Irish " [:a {:href "/athletes/920792"} "Oliver St. John Gogarty"] ", for " [:em {} "Ode to the Tailteann Games"] ". Gonnet also played rugby for the French national team from 1921-27. In 1924, he was a " [:a {:href "/results/31513"} "substitute for the French national team"] " at the Paris Olympics, but did not play in any match. He was also an athlete, swimmer, and boxer. Besides his writing, Gogarty was a physician, sportsman, pilot, and politician, and, from 1922-36, served as a senator in the Irish Parliament."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Catalogue Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Translated Title"] [:th {} "Original Title"] [:th {} "Title in Olympic Report or Catalogue"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/920285"} " Géo-Ch